    "case_summary": "A woman in her early 30s with a 10-year history of treatment with various medications (glatiramer acetate, interferon beta, fingolimod, natalizumab, and most recently dimethyl fumarate for 3 years). No history of severe lymphocytopenia. At last outpatient visit, she had impaired extraocular movement and mild sinking of the right leg. A few months later, she developed dizziness and nausea. Five days after receiving methylprednisolone treatment, she presented with moderate tetraparesis, headache, fever, and neck stiffness. Prior to neurological symptoms, her entire family had experienced fever, nausea, vomiting, and diarrhea, recovering within 3 days, while the patient continued to have mild gastrointestinal symptoms.\n\nNeurologic examination revealed:- Positive Romberg test- Impaired extraocular movement- Mild sinking of right leg at position test- Moderate tetraparesis- Neck stiffness- Fever\n\n- Laboratory tests:  * Initial white blood cell count: 4900/μL (lymphocyte count: 1240/μL)  * Later white blood cell count: 18,000/μL (lymphocyte count: 780/μL)  * Cerebrospinal fluid analysis: Clear fluid, normal glucose level, increased protein level, 108 cells/mL (95% neutrophils)  * Cerebrospinal fluid culture: Positive- Imaging studies:  * MRI Title: Brain MRI at hospital admission  * MRI Description: T2 fluid-attenuated inversion recovery showed hyperintense lesion in the brainstem and cerebellum with slight contrast enhancement    * Follow-up MRI Title: Brain MRI at discharge  * Follow-up MRI Description: Reduced signal change on T2-weighted images, increased contrast enhancement showing ringlike appearance",
